      ":question/id": "8883bfa3-8f36-5e8a-8a63-990442c2b246",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120087",
      ":question/problem": "$10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({150}^{∘})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({150}^{∘}) & =5\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({75}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186233283,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
      ":question/id": "19bb82ee-2583-57ae-92ec-48971505d385",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120094",
      ":question/problem": "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} & =\\frac{4}{2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
