      ":question/id": "87e7afa8-1798-5127-a9ff-a08924db07de",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-24792",
      ":question/problem": "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
