      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
      ":question/id": "4fabc70d-25ca-5a30-9a52-90a915524efa",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-328087",
      ":question/problem": "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
