      ":question/id": "f8fa23a0-df4e-59d2-a1e6-1e3769930383",
      ":question/problem": "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of\n$$\n8\\,\\sin\\left(15^\\circ\\right)\\cos\\left(15^\\circ\\right)\\cos\\left(30^\\circ\\right).\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\\sin x\\cos x=\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(2x)$ first at $x=15^\\circ$ and then at $x=30^\\circ$. Each application introduces a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=4\\,\\sin\\left(30^\\circ\\right)\\cos\\left(30^\\circ\\right)\\\\&=2\\,\\sin\\left(60^\\circ\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe terminal sine is $\\sin\\left(60^\\circ\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=2\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\&=\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186059500,
    ":question/id": "35535b08-b3d8-5037-a821-6815577169d2",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3618",
    ":question/problem": "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)}.\n$$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\right]\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\left[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$"
  },
